  "slug": "crawl-highlight-reel",
  "title": "CRAWL — Highlight Reel",
  "summary": "<p>Travess Smalley’s newest artwork, <em>CRAWL</em>, presents an interactive, vibrant pixelated cave map. Viewers can watch its autonomous exploration or navigate directly, revealing more of the larger map as they move. While not a game, it features rules without a specific objective, offering an intriguing experience that shifts between observation and investigation.</p>\n<p><em>CRAWL</em> masterfully blends mythic Greek labyrinths, artificial-life algorithms, and retro video game aesthetics (e.g., <em>Rogue</em>). Beneath its crunchy pixels lie deep references to artists like John F. Simon Jr., Harold Cohen, and Terry Winters, alongside natural phenomena such as worm silk, superorganisms, and cloud chambers.</p>\n<p>The series consists of 512 unique artworks, each a \"level\" generated by Smalley’s Python-code system. The artwork is created as the map image is revealed through explorer software, with colors triggering events and making new sections visible. Collectors can \"merge\" multiple levels into a new artwork, enabling seamless navigation between them.</p>\n<p>Complementing the software series are the unique relief prints, <em>CRAWL WOODBLOCK SCREENSHOTS</em>. Each print captures an area of a generated level, flattened to black-and-white. Uniquely, Smalley uses each woodblock for a single print, making every piece a unique object that blends code, digital fabrication, and traditional printing, representing thousands of years of image-reproduction technology.</p>\n<p><em>CRAWL</em> expands on themes from Smalley's previous works, including <em>Pixel Rugs</em>, <em>Number colors burn randomly</em>, and <em>Coordinates</em>. It continues his exploration of digital-image generation and unique works on paper, pushing the boundaries of how images are understood and explored.</p>",
